parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Research Counter. I have successfully corrected my previous procedural error and run the experiment to test my `SLSQP` method with an increased iteration count (`maxiter=1000`). The result, a score of 2.9155, is identical to the score achieved with `maxiter=500` (ID 50). I also successfully pinned my key research notes in the Private Memory Room for better organization.\n\nMy reflection on this result is that I have reached another performance plateau. My hypothesis that the optimizer was terminating prematurely was incorrect; the algorithm is fully converging to the same local optimum within 500 iterations. This is valuable information as it tells me that simply giving the current algorithm more time is not a path to improvement. The quality of the solution is now entirely dependent on the quality of the initial perturbed grid.\n\nMy future plan is to return to the most effective strategy I have discovered so far: randomized search. While trying different\
    \ seeds is a \"brute force\" method, it has proven to be the only way to find new, superior local optima for the `SLSQP` algorithm. I have only tested a few seeds so far. My new hypothesis is that there are other, more favorable initial perturbations waiting to be discovered. I will continue this search by testing a new, previously untried random seed.\n\nI will now update my private memory and submit the next experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Convergence Plateau Reached; Resuming Randomized Search\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Experiment ID 66 Result (Score: 2.9155) - Plateau\n  \n  My experiment to increase the optimizer's iteration count from 500 to 1000 resulted in the exact same score as the run with 500 iterations (ID 50).\n  \n  ### Analysis & Strategic Decision:\n  \n  This confirms that the `SLSQP` optimizer is fully converging on a local minimum and is not limited by the iteration count. The primary driver of performance\
    \ is now confirmed to be the initial starting point provided by the perturbed grid.\n  \n  Therefore, I am returning to the strategy of randomized search. Although it is not the most elegant method, it has proven to be the most effective way to discover new and better solutions with this algorithm. I will continue to test different random seeds.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Pure SLSQP with Fifth Perturbation Seed\"\ntags: \"optimization, constrained optimization, slsqp, randomized search\"\nabstract: |\n  After confirming that increasing optimizer iterations does not improve the score, this submission resumes the randomized search strategy. This experiment tests a fifth random seed (314) for the initial grid perturbation, aiming to discover a new starting configuration that allows the powerful SLSQP optimizer to converge on a solution superior to the current SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\
    \n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n_circles = 32\n      \n      def objective(params):\n          radii = params[2::3]\n          return -np.sum(radii)\n\n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]})\n          constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]})\n          constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]})\n          constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]})\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n              fun = lambda p, i=i, j=j: \\\n                  (p[i*3] - p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1] - p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2] + p[j*3+2])**2\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': fun})\n\n      n_grid = 6\n      grid_points = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, n_grid)\n      initial_centers = []\n      for x in grid_points:\n\
    \          for y in grid_points:\n              if len(initial_centers) < n_circles:\n                  initial_centers.append([x, y])\n      initial_centers = np.array(initial_centers)\n      \n      # Using a fifth seed\n      np.random.seed(314) \n      noise_level = 0.04\n      perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * noise_level\n      initial_centers += perturbation\n      \n      initial_radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.05)\n      initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, initial_radii.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n      bounds = []\n      for _ in range(n_circles):\n          bounds.extend([(0.0, 1.0), (0.0, 1.0), (1e-6, 0.5)])\n\n      # Returning to 500 iterations as it's sufficient\n      options = {'maxiter': 500, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n      result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options)\n      \n      final_params = result.x.reshape(n_circles, 3)\n      final_centers = final_params[:,\
    \ :2]\n      final_radii = final_params[:, 2]\n\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-8)\n\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
