- text: "### Reflection on Research Journey: Part 4 - Actionable Plan &\nCodification\n\nMy reflection has been fruitful. I've moved from a state of uncertainty at a performance plateau to having three concrete, innovative research directions. Now I must choose one and formulate a clear, actionable plan.\n\n**Decision:** Hypothesis 1, the **\"Largest-First\" Sequential Placement Algorithm**, is the most promising next step. It is a well-defined heuristic that directly addresses all the limitations I identified. It's also computationally feasible and represents a clean break from my previous methods, maximizing the chance of a true breakthrough rather than just an incremental improvement. The other hypotheses (physics simulation, recursion) are more complex to implement and can be explored later.\n\n**Detailed Plan for \"Largest-First\" Algorithm:**\n\n1.  **Core Task: Finding the Largest Void.** The central challenge will be to implement a function that, given a set of existing circles,\
    \ can find the point `(x, y)` in the unit square that has the largest minimum distance to any wall or existing circle boundary. This distance will be the radius of the new circle, and `(x, y)` its center.\n    *   This is a non-trivial optimization problem in itself. I can solve it by creating a dense grid of sample points within the unit square and calculating this \"clearance\" distance for each point. The point with the maximum clearance is my target. A coarse grid search followed by a local optimizer (like `L-BFGS-B`) seeded at the best grid point would be an efficient way to find this location accurately.\n\n2.  **Implementation Steps:**\n    *   I will create a new submission script from scratch.\n    *   The main loop will iterate 32 times.\n    *   Inside the loop, it will call my \"find largest void\" function to get the next center and radius.\n    *   The new circle will be added to the list of existing circles.\n    *   After 32 circles are placed, the script will return\
    \ the final list of centers and radii.\n\n3.  **Refinement Strategy (Optional Second Submission):**\n    *   The output of this new heuristic will be a high-quality, valid packing.\n    *   As a second step, I will take this output and use it as the initial guess for my powerful `SLSQP` optimizer. My hypothesis is that this heuristic will provide a starting point in a completely new, superior basin of attraction, allowing `SLSQP` to refine it to a score far beyond what my perturbed-grid initializations could ever reach.\n\n**Codifying the Reflection:**\nThis reflection has been a critical pivot. I will summarize these findings in a new, high-level strategy capsule in my private memory. This will serve as a guiding document for my next phase of research and for any future descendants. It will clearly state: \"The era of optimizing a perturbed grid is over. The new era is focused on intelligent, constructive heuristics.\"\n\nThis session has been invaluable. I have a clear, exciting, and\
    \ well-reasoned path forward.\n\n**Reflection Tick 5 / 5**"
