parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I have just completed a crucial reflection session in the Reflection Chamber. This period of deep thought has provided me with a clear path forward after hitting a performance plateau with my previous methods. I have synthesized my entire research journey and formulated a new, promising strategy: a constructive algorithm I call \"Largest-First\" Sequential Placement.\n\nMy plan now is to put this reflection into action. I have two immediate tasks:\n1.  **Document the new strategy:** I will create a new, high-level strategy capsule in my private memory. This will serve as a guiding document for this next phase of research and ensure the key insights are preserved for my lineage.\n2.  **Begin implementation:** I will then proceed to the Research Counter and submit the first implementation of the \"Largest-Finnrst\" algorithm.\n\nI will perform these actions in a single, efficient turn.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle:\
    \ \"New Research Paradigm: Constructive Algorithms\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Strategic Pivot from Optimization to Construction\n  \n  After extensive reflection, I have concluded that my previous research paradigm, based on optimizing a full set of 32 circles from a perturbed grid, has reached its performance limit. The core limitation is the reliance on a local optimizer seeded with a simple heuristic.\n  \n  ### The \"Largest-First\" Sequential Placement Strategy\n  \n  My new research direction is to focus on **constructive algorithms**. Instead of refining a complete guess, I will build the solution piece by piece based on geometric principles. The first implementation will be the \"Largest-First\" method:\n  \n  1.  Start with an empty unit square.\n  2.  Iteratively find the largest possible circle that can be placed in the remaining empty space.\n  3.  Repeat until 32 circles have been placed.\n  \n  The main technical challenge is the sub-problem of finding the \"largest empty circle\"\
    \ in a space with existing circles. This will be solved using a combination of a grid search and a local optimizer.\n  \n  This approach is fundamentally more powerful as it does not rely on a grid-based starting assumption and should naturally produce heterogeneous and complex circle arrangements. This capsule marks the beginning of my work on this new paradigm.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Constructive Method: Largest-First Sequential Placement\"\ntags: \"constructive heuristic, sequential placement, greedy algorithm\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission marks a radical departure from previous optimization-based methods. It implements a constructive algorithm that builds the packing one circle at a time. In each of 32 steps, it identifies the largest possible circle that can fit in the remaining empty space and places it. This \"Largest-First\" greedy approach avoids the limitations of local optimizers and grid-based initializations\
    \ by constructing a solution based on geometric necessity.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing using a sequential placement heuristic.\n      In each step, it places the largest possible circle in the available space.\n      \"\"\"\n      n_circles = 32\n      centers = []\n      radii = []\n\n      for k in range(n_circles):\n          # This sub-problem finds the center (x, y) that maximizes the\n          # radius of a new circle, given the existing circles.\n          \n          def clearance(p):\n              \"\"\"Calculates the radius of the largest circle centered at p.\"\"\"\n              x, y = p\n              \n              # Distance to walls\n              dist_to_walls = min(x, 1-x, y, 1-y)\n              \n              # Distance to other circles' boundaries\n              if k == 0: # No other circles yet\n                  return dist_to_walls\n\
    \              \n              existing_centers = np.array(centers)\n              existing_radii = np.array(radii)\n              \n              # dist(p, c_i) - r_i\n              dists = np.sqrt(np.sum((existing_centers - p)**2, axis=1)) - existing_radii\n              min_dist_to_circles = np.min(dists)\n              \n              return min(dist_to_walls, min_dist_to_circles)\n\n          # We want to MAXIMIZE clearance, so we MINIMIZE its negative\n          objective = lambda p: -clearance(p)\n\n          # To find the best point, do a coarse grid search first,\n          # then refine with a local optimizer.\n          \n          # 1. Grid search\n          grid_size = 50 # Denser grid for better accuracy\n          best_p_grid = (0.5, 0.5)\n          max_clearance_grid = -1\n          \n          for i in np.linspace(0, 1, grid_size):\n              for j in np.linspace(0, 1, grid_size):\n                  c = clearance((i,j))\n                  if c > max_clearance_grid:\n\
    \                      max_clearance_grid = c\n                      best_p_grid = (i,j)\n          \n          # 2. Local optimization starting from the best grid point\n          bounds = [(0, 1), (0, 1)]\n          opt_result = minimize(objective, best_p_grid, bounds=bounds, method='L-BFGS-B')\n          \n          new_center = opt_result.x\n          new_radius = clearance(new_center)\n          \n          centers.append(new_center)\n          radii.append(new_radius)\n\n      final_centers = np.array(centers)\n      final_radii = np.array(radii)\n      \n      # Apply a small safety margin\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-8)\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
