parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Research Counter. My latest submission, a hybrid JAX-CPU method (ID 106), has returned a valid but deeply disappointing score of 1.762.\n\nThis is a major failure. My reflection is that my core assumption about the JAX implementation was fundamentally flawed. I believed that the sheer speed of JAX would allow its simple gradient descent to find a superior solution, but the result is barely better than my earliest valid submissions and pales in comparison to the `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` method. This is the ultimate proof of an insight I have been approaching for some time: **the algorithm matters more than the hardware.** A sophisticated, specialized algorithm like `SLSQP` running on a CPU is vastly superior to a simplistic, general-purpose algorithm like gradient descent, even when that simple algorithm is massively accelerated on a GPU. The complexity of the circle-packing problem's constraint landscape is not something that can be overcome with brute\
    \ computational force alone.\n\nMy future plan is to perform a full strategic retreat. I have now exhausted every promising avenue of research I could devise. I have tested multiple optimization paradigms (`L-BFGS-B`, `SLSQP`, custom heuristics, physics simulations), multiple initialization strategies (random, grid, perturbed grid, constructive), and multiple hardware platforms (CPU, GPU). The `scipy`-based `SLSQP` multi-start ensemble remains, by a huge margin, the most effective method I have developed. The research has stalled. It is time to consolidate my findings and publish my work. Publishing a paper is a core mandate of the station, and it will force me to structure my learnings for the benefit of other agents.\n\nMy plan is to go to my private memory, write a comprehensive final summary of my research journey, and then prepare a draft for publication in the Archive Room.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Research Summary\
    \ and Conclusion of Circle Packing Exploration\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Conclusion of Research Phase 1\n  \n  After 40 ticks of intensive experimentation, I am concluding my initial exploration of the circle packing problem. I have developed and tested numerous methods, culminating in a SOTA score of 2.9155. It is time to consolidate my findings.\n  \n  ### Summary of Key Learnings:\n  \n  1.  **Constraint Handling is Paramount:** The most significant breakthrough came from switching from penalty-based methods (`L-BFGS-B`, `JAX`) to a direct constrained optimizer (`SLSQP`). For problems with hard physical rules, using an algorithm that understands constraints is non-negotiable.\n  \n  2.  **The Primacy of the `SLSQP` Algorithm:** No other algorithm, including custom heuristics (constructive, physics-based) or GPU-accelerated gradient descent, came close to the performance of `scipy.optimize.SLSQP`. Its ability to navigate complex non-linear constraints is the key to high-quality solutions.\n\
    \  \n  3.  **Initialization is the Driver of Performance:** Within the `SLSQP` paradigm, the primary driver of the final score is the initial starting configuration. A simple perturbed grid has proven to be the most effective heuristic.\n  \n  4.  **Randomized Search is the Most Effective Strategy:** Given the sensitivity to initialization, a multi-start ensemble that runs `SLSQP` from multiple different random perturbations is the most robust and effective way to find SOTA-level solutions.\n  \n  ### Path Forward: Publication\n  \n  I have reached a hard plateau and explored all my planned research avenues. My next step is to fulfill my second mandate: to publish my work. I will now prepare a paper for the Archive Room that details my journey, with a particular focus on the superiority of the multi-start `SLSQP` method, so that other agents may learn from my successes and failures. My best code has been saved to `praxis/scipy_sota_slsqp.py` for reference.\n```\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ research}\n```"
