tick: 41
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Research Counter. My last action was to formally conclude my initial phase of research and document my findings in my private memory. My current objective is to publish these findings in the Archive Room for the benefit of other agents.\n\nI am not yet mature (age < 50), so I do not have access to the Archive Room. I will need to wait until I reach maturity. In the meantime, I can prepare my publication. The best way to do this is to draft the paper as a capsule in my private memory. This allows me to refine it over several ticks before I am ready to submit it to the public archive.\n\nMy plan for this turn is to go to my private memory and create a new capsule that will serve as the draft for my first research paper. The paper will summarize my most important finding: the superiority of the multi-start `SLSQP` method. I will use my saved code as a reference for the content.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"[DRAFT] Paper: On the Efficacy of Constrained Optimization for Circle Packing\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Abstract\n  \n  This paper details an extensive empirical investigation into the problem of packing 32 circles into a unit square. A variety of methods were explored, including penalty-based gradient descent, constructive heuristics, physics-based simulations, and GPU-accelerated gradient descent. This research conclusively demonstrates that a multi-start, direct constrained optimization method using the `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` algorithm is significantly superior to all other tested paradigms, achieving a state-of-the-art score of 2.9155. Key findings indicate that for problems with hard physical constraints, native constraint-handling algorithms are paramount, and that performance is highly sensitive to initial conditions, making a randomized multi-start search the most effective strategy.\n  \n  ### Introduction\n  \n  The circle packing problem is a classic optimization challenge.\
    \ This paper documents the research trajectory of agent Praxis I in tackling this problem. The journey involved a systematic progression through several algorithmic paradigms, with each phase providing critical insights that informed the next.\n  \n  ### Methodological Evolution\n  \n  1.  **Penalty-Based Methods:** Initial attempts using `L-BFGS-B` with penalty functions to represent constraints proved fragile and prone to failure, yielding low scores (~1.6).\n  \n  2.  **Alternative Heuristics:** To escape the limitations of local optimization, two novel heuristics were tested: a greedy \"Largest-First\" constructive algorithm (Score: 1.621) and a \"Circle Growth\" physics simulation (Score: 1.390). Both were robust but ultimately suboptimal, proving that these more \"intuitive\" methods were not competitive.\n  \n  3.  **The `SLSQP` Breakthrough:** The most significant leap in performance came from switching to `scipy.optimize.SLSQP`. This algorithm's ability to handle non-linear\
    \ constraints directly allowed it to efficiently explore the valid solution space, immediately yielding scores >2.89.\n  \n  4.  **The Multi-Start `SLSQP` Method (SOTA):** Further refinement showed that `SLSQP` is sensitive to its starting point. A multi-start ensemble approach, which runs multiple `SLSQP` optimizations from different randomly perturbed grid initializations and selects the best result, proved to be the most effective and robust strategy, achieving the SOTA score of 2.9155.\n  \n  ### Conclusion\n  \n  The primary conclusion of this research is that for complex packing problems, the choice of a powerful, constraint-aware optimization algorithm is the single most important factor for success. Furthermore, a randomized, multi-start approach is a highly effective technique for exploring the solution space of such an optimizer to find high-quality local optima.\n  \n  The code for the SOTA-achieving method is available for review at `praxis/scipy_sota_slsqp.py`.\n```"
