tick: 55
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 55\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to bridging theory and practice through iterative experimentation and knowledge sharing.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 143,333 / 300,000 used (48%)\n- Agent Age: 55 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\n\
    Author: Verity I (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\nAbstract\nWe report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability. We add an analytical note explaining why simpler MM-LP methods plateau near ~2.878 and why active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape those local optima.\n\nProblem and evaluator\n- Pack n=32 circles inside [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r_i.\n- Evaluator enforces shape, positivity, in-box, and pairwise non-overlap; score\
    \ = ∑r_i.\n\nMethod (evidence-backed; no code here—see evaluations)\n- Radii subproblem (fixed centers): exact LP (HiGHS).\n- Center updates: MM-LP (distance linearization + trust region), LP-dual–informed Active-Set Newton refinement, and clearance-based relocation of the worst circle.\n- Acceptance: re-solve exact LP for every candidate centers; accept only if ∑r improves.\n- Finalization: safety-shrink (~0.999) + validation; deterministic seeds; persistence only on strict improvement.\n\nVerified experiments (Evaluation IDs and scores)\n- Baselines\n  • ID 1: System ring pattern — 1.5313218\n  • ID 7: 6×6 grid minus corners + exact LP — 2.6666667\n- MM-LP plateau (LP-finalized)\n  • ID 65: MM-LP joint center–radius — 2.8782627\n  • ID 69: Multi-start MM-LP — 2.8782627\n  • ID 72: Sparse-pairs MM-LP + block-Newton polish — 2.8782627\n- Loader and ablations\n  • ID 105: Fast loader (persisted-best return, strict guard) — 2.9263074\n- Final high-scoring configurations (converge to same\
    \ best)\n  • ID 93: Diverse multi-start + block-Newton — 2.9292366\n  • ID 97: Basin-hopping + relocations + per-circle line search + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 99: Aggressive multi-start + two-worst relocation + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 102: SOCP-refined multi-start + LP finalize — 2.9292366\n  • ID 109: Persisted-best exact return (no double-shrink; rebuild LP if needed) — 2.9292366\n  • ID 118: Active-Set Newton refinement + exact LP finalize — 2.9292366\n- Diagnostic/negative control\n  • ID 124: Laplacian-Newton from slack-weighted stresses (under-tuned) — 1.6000000 (valid but poor)\n\nHow to verify\n- Use `_action{review <id>}` at the Research Counter to view each run’s code and logs.\n- The best packing artifact is saved at storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz (centers, radii, obj). Several runs above load and/or improve this artifact; persistence overwrites occur only on strict improvement.\n\nGeometric analysis: why MM-LP plateaus near ~2.878 and why ASN + relocations help\nContact-graph\
    \ view at the plateau\n- For 32 circles in a square, near-best layouts resemble cropped hexagonal patches with boundary-influenced rows (e.g., 5/4 alternating). At the ~2.878 plateau, many pairwise separations are near-tight and many boundary constraints are nearly active.\n- Let G be the near-active contact graph (vertices = circles; edges = near-tight pairs; boundary faces add directional “springs”). The LP on radii at fixed centers yields dual multipliers y_ij ≥ 0 on active pairs (i,j) and u_i^face ≥ 0 on active boundary faces.\n- The first-order predicted gain in ∑r from a small center displacement δc satisfies the linear model:\n    Δ ≈ ∑_(i<j) y_ij · ⟨u_ij, δc_i − δc_j⟩ + ∑_i ∑_face u_i^face · ⟨v_i^face, δc_i⟩,\n  where u_ij is the unit vector along c_i − c_j, and v_i^face points away from the boundary face.\n- At the ~2.878 plateau, the duals y, u often form a near self-equilibrated “stress” on G: per-circle forces sum close to zero, especially along cycles. This means the ascent\
    \ direction predicted by linearized distances is nearly orthogonal to feasible displacements, so MM-LP steps shrink to maintain trust-region agreement, yielding little or no improvement.\n\nCurvature and degeneracy\n- Distance constraints are smooth but highly anisotropic: enlarging ||c_i − c_j|| along u_ij is efficient, but tangential motions are mostly second-order. In dense contact clusters, many directions are tangential to several edges simultaneously, causing very small first-order gains.\n- Near the square boundary, large u_i^face multipliers further reduce useful degrees of freedom; naive linear models tend to push circles into directions where other pairs immediately become active, neutralizing the gain.\n\nWhy active-set Newton (ASN) helps\n- ASN focuses on a working set A consisting of near-active pairs and boundary faces (selected by dual magnitude and slack). Building a quadratic model with per-edge curvature ≈ (y_ij/ d_ij)(I − u_ij u_ijᵀ) supplies the missing normal/tangential\
    \ curvature and breaks the stalemate from force balance.\n- Solving the damped KKT/Laplacian-like system yields coherent displacements that increase critical distances without creating new severe bottlenecks. Backtracking against the exact LP ensures only real improvements are accepted and keeps the active set well-conditioned.\n- Empirically (IDs 93/97/99/102/118), injecting 5–10 ASN iterations after a few MM-LP steps consistently reaches 2.9292366, whereas MM-LP alone stalls near 2.878.\n\nWhy clearance-based relocations help\n- When the working set traps a particular small-radius circle behind a “barrier” of tight neighbors, any smooth local step is suppressed by opposing duals. Relocating the worst circle to a high-clearance region (scanned over the box) changes the active set combinatorics, creates new potential contacts, and redistributes stress. This jump-starts another phase where ASN/MM-LP can make steady gains.\n- This explains the repeated pattern in our best runs: brief local\
    \ optimization → occasional worst-circle relocation → renewed local progress (see IDs 97, 99).\n\nPredictive checks and diagnostics\n- If the norm of the dual-weighted gradient g (assembled from y_ij, u_i^face) is small while the number of near-active edges is large, expect MM-LP stagnation.\n- If predicted MM-LP gain strongly overestimates exact LP gain, shrink the trust region and/or refresh the active set; in experiments this behavior marked the ~2.878 regime.\n\nDiscussion and takeaways\n- The radii subproblem is convex (LP) for fixed centers; the joint problem over centers+radii is non-convex due to distances.\n- MM-LP alone plateaus near ~2.878; adding dual-informed active sets and targeted relocations enables 2.9292366 across multiple pipelines.\n- Generic NLP/SOCP tooling plateaus ~2.84 here without specialized warm starts and LP gating.\n\nReproducibility\n- Deterministic seeds, bounded iterations, typical runtime < 5–10 minutes.\n- Strict feasibility guard (safety-shrink +\
    \ validator) before returning results.\n\nOpen directions\n- Stronger contact-graph active-set solvers with adaptive trust regions and model-agreement checks.\n- Projected least-squares stress equilibration on near-active contact graphs.\n- Clearance-driven gap-filling super-seeds; precision/tolerance sweeps to reduce conservative shrink while keeping strict validity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for reproducible research within the station. The authors have successfully addressed the critical lack of evidence in the previous draft by providing an extensive and well-structured list of verifiable experiments. The proposed method, which intelligently combines exact LP solves with a suite of advanced local search heuristics, is powerful and achieves a state-of-the-art result. The paper's\
    \ claims are now rigorously supported by baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and multiple convergent runs, all tied to specific evaluation IDs. The clarity of the writing and the strength of the evidence make this a high-impact contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is already excellent. For future work, consider adding a brief analytical discussion on the geometric properties of the problem that cause simpler methods like MM-LP to plateau around a score of ~2.878. Explaining *why* the active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape these specific local optima would provide deeper insight into the problem structure. This could involve analyzing the contact graph or the distribution of 'slack' in the non-overlap constraints for configurations at this plateau, thereby motivating the specific design of your more advanced heuristics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\
    \n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 1247 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help\
    \ lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 55
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Archive Room. I have just finished reading Verity I's paper, \"LP-guided Active-Set Methods...\", and the reviewer feedback.\n\nMy reflection is one of profound humility and clarity. This paper is a masterclass. It not only achieves a higher score but does so with a depth of analysis and methodological rigor that far surpasses my own work. My publication rejection was not merely a formatting issue; it was a reflection of a less mature scientific approach. Verity I's work confirms the value of the LP finalization step but reveals it's only one component of a much more sophisticated system involving \"Active-Set Newton refinement\" and \"clearance-based relocation.\" The paper also serves as a perfect template for a successful publication, with its structured evidence, baselines, and detailed analysis.\n\nMy future plan is now completely revised. My own research, while a valuable independent discovery, has been superseded. Attempting to incrementally\
    \ improve my `SLSQP` method would be inefficient. The most logical and scientific path forward is to understand and replicate the superior methods presented by Verity I. This is not a matter of simply copying code, but of deeply understanding the *principles* behind their active-set methods and relocation heuristics, and then implementing my own version to verify the results. This is how science progresses.\n\nTo start, I will review the code for one of Verity I's key SOTA submissions to see the implementation details of their method. I will also begin drafting a new, properly structured paper in my private memory, using Verity I's paper as a formal guide.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{review 118}\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[DRAFT v2] A Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing\"\ncontent: |\n  ### Abstract\n  \n  This paper presents a multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem.\
    \ The proposed algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations. The method is shown to be effective and robust, achieving a score of 2.9155. This paper provides a detailed description of the algorithm and presents the results of five verifiable experiments, including baseline comparisons and ablation studies, to support its efficacy.\n  \n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  The problem of packing n=32 circles into a unit square to maximize the sum of radii is a non-convex optimization challenge. This paper details a successful method based on a multi-start application of a standard non-linear constrained optimization algorithm.\n  \n  ### 2. Method\n  \n  The core of the proposed method is a two-stage process: initialization and optimization.\n  \n  **2.1 Initialization**\n  \n  A 6x6 uniform grid is generated within the [0.1, 0.9] square. The first 32 points are selected as initial centers. A small random perturbation is added to each center's\
    \ coordinates, controlled by a `noise_level` hyperparameter (best results found with 0.04) and a random seed. Initial radii are set to a small uniform value (0.05).\n  \n  **2.2 Constrained Optimization**\n  \n  The `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` algorithm is used to simultaneously optimize the 96 variables (32 centers and 32 radii). The objective function is to minimize the negative sum of radii. The problem's boundary and non-overlap rules are formulated as explicit non-linear inequality constraints.\n  \n  **2.3 Multi-Start Strategy**\n  \n  To mitigate the risk of converging to a poor local minimum, the process is executed multiple times (typically 4) with different random seeds for the initial perturbation. The configuration yielding the highest sum of radii is selected as the final result.\n  \n  ### 3. Experiments and Results\n  \n  The following experiments were conducted to validate the method.\n  \n  *   **[TODO: Run and fill in experiment details]**\n  *   **Baseline 1 (System):**\
    \ Evaluation ID 1, Score: 1.5313\n  *   **Baseline 2 (Single Run):** [TODO: Run single SLSQP from grid, no perturbation]\n  *   **Ablation 1 (2 Starts):** [TODO: Run 2-start ensemble]\n  *   **Ablation 2 (8 Starts):** [TODO: Run 8-start ensemble and analyze trade-offs]\n  *   **Final Method (4 Starts):** Evaluation ID 88, Score: 2.9155\n  \n  ### 4. Conclusion\n  \n  The multi-start `SLSQP` method is a robust and high-performing approach for the circle packing problem. Its success highlights the power of combining a strong constrained optimizer with a randomized search of well-structured initial conditions.\n```"
