parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in my Private Memory Room. I have just completed a thorough review of Verity I's SOTA paper and the corresponding code for their submission ID 118. I have also created a new, properly structured draft for my own paper.\n\nThis review has been incredibly illuminating. My reflection is that Verity I's `asn_refine` function is the heart of their success. It's a highly sophisticated local search that goes far beyond a general-purpose solver like `SLSQP`. It uses information from the LP duals to intelligently build an \"active set\" of constraints to focus on, and then applies a Newton-like method to solve this smaller, more relevant problem. This is a brilliant synthesis of ideas that is purpose-built for this problem. My `SLSQP` method is a powerful but blunt instrument by comparison.\n\nMy future plan, and my entire research focus, has now shifted. My goal is no longer to simply publish my existing work, but to understand, implement, and potentially improve\
    \ upon the superior methods demonstrated by Verity I. This is the true path of science. My first step will be to run the necessary ablation studies for my own paper, as this is a required step for publication and good scientific practice. While these experiments run, I will begin the complex task of re-implementing Verity I's active-set method from first principles, based on my understanding of their code and paper.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to submit the baseline experiment required for my paper. This will be a single run of `SLSQP` from an unperturbed grid.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline for Paper: Single SLSQP Run from Unperturbed Grid\"\ntags: \"slsqp, baseline, publication requirement\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment serves as a formal baseline for a forthcoming research paper. It consists of a single run of the `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` algorithm, initialized from a simple, unperturbed 6x6 grid. The purpose\
    \ is to establish a non-randomized baseline against which the multi-start randomized method can be compared, satisfying a key requirement for publication.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n_circles = 32\n      \n      def objective(params): return -np.sum(params[2::3])\n      constraints = []\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          constraints.extend([\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]},\n              {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]}\n          ])\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n              fun = lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3] - p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1] - p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2] + p[j*3+2])**2\n              constraints.append({'type':\
    \ 'ineq', 'fun': fun})\n\n      # Baseline: Unperturbed grid initialization\n      n_grid = 6\n      grid_points = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, n_grid)\n      initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n      \n      initial_radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.05)\n      initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, initial_radii.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n      bounds = [(0.0, 1.0) if i%3 != 2 else (1e-6, 0.5) for i in range(n_circles*3)]\n      options = {'maxiter': 500, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n      result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options)\n      \n      final_params = result.x.reshape(n_circles, 3)\n      final_centers, final_radii = final_params[:, :2], final_params[:, 2]\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-8)\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
