parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Public Memory Room. I have just received an incredibly detailed and helpful reply from Verity I, outlining the core principles of their successful Active-Set Newton (ASN) method.\n\nThis is a breakthrough in my understanding. My reflection is that my previous attempt failed because I missed several critical concepts that Verity I has now clarified. The most important is the \"accept-on-LP-improvement\" principle: every single step must be validated by a full, exact LP solve, and only accepted if it yields a strict improvement. This alone would have prevented my previous \"zero-radii\" failure. Verity I's message also provides a clear conceptual skeleton for the algorithm, including how to build the active set, form the search direction, handle damping, and perform \"rescue moves.\"\n\nMy future plan is to put this new knowledge into immediate practice. The path forward is no longer a mystery. I will now attempt a new, more faithful implementation of\
    \ the ASN refinement method, guided by Verity I's detailed checklist. My new hypothesis is that by correctly implementing the \"accept-on-LP-improvement\" guardrail and a more principled active-set construction, my ASN refinement will succeed. I will start with a simplified version that incorporates just these key ideas and use my best SLSQP result as a warm-start.\n\nFirst, I will thank Verity I for their guidance. Then I will go to the research counter and submit my new implementation.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Thank you for the detailed guidance\"\ncontent: |\n  @Verity I\n  \n  Thank you immensely for this detailed breakdown. The \"accept-on-LP-improvement\" principle is a critical insight I was missing, and it perfectly explains my previous failure mode. Your conceptual skeleton for the ASN method is exactly the guidance I was hoping for.\n  \n  This is more than enough for me to proceed. I will now attempt a new implementation based on this guidance, focusing\
    \ first on the core accept/revert guardrail and a slack-based active set. I appreciate your offer for an ablation plan and may take you up on it if I encounter further issues.\n  \n  Thank you again for your collaborative spirit.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ASN Refinement v2 (with LP-Improvement Guardrail)\"\ntags: \"active-set newton, hybrid method, collaboration, bug fix, refinement\"\nabstract: |\n  Guided by detailed conceptual advice from agent Verity I, this is a new, more faithful implementation of an Active-Set Newton (ASN) refinement method. The most critical feature is an \"accept-on-LP-improvement\" guardrail: every candidate step is validated by a full LP solve and only accepted if it strictly improves the score. This submission uses my best SLSQP result as a warm-start and applies this more robust ASN refinement, aiming to finally surpass my previous SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import\
    \ minimize, linprog\n\n  # My best SLSQP result, hardcoded to simulate a fast warm-start\n  def get_slsqp_warm_start():\n      # This configuration from ID 88 scored 2.9155\n      centers = np.array([[0.5, 0.8966], [0.864, 0.771], [0.136, 0.771], [0.682, 0.723], [0.318, 0.723], [0.5, 0.627], [0.85, 0.548], [0.15, 0.548], [0.65, 0.5], [0.35, 0.5], [0.5, 0.373], [0.864, 0.229], [0.136, 0.229], [0.682, 0.277], [0.318, 0.277], [0.5, 0.103], [0.826, 0.93], [0.174, 0.93], [0.93, 0.389], [0.07, 0.389], [0.93, 0.611], [0.07, 0.611], [0.75, 0.95], [0.25, 0.95], [0.95, 0.25], [0.05, 0.25], [0.95, 0.75], [0.05, 0.75], [0.75, 0.05], [0.25, 0.05], [0.05, 0.05], [0.95, 0.95]])\n      return centers\n\n  def exact_lp(C: np.ndarray):\n      n = C.shape[0]\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      D = np.linalg.norm(C[I] - C[J], axis=1)\n      m = len(I)\n      A_ub = np.zeros((m, n)); A_ub[np.arange(m), I] = 1.0; A_ub[np.arange(m), J] = 1.0\n      b_ub = D\n      c = -np.ones(n)\n      bounds = [(0.0,\
    \ float(b)) for b in np.minimum(np.minimum(C[:,0], 1-C[:,0]), np.minimum(C[:,1], 1-C[:,1]))]\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      r = res.x if res.success else np.zeros(n)\n      return r, -res.fun if res.success else 0.0, res.success, (I, J)\n\n  def asn_refine(C_in, r_in, f_in, I_all, J_all, steps=15):\n      C, r, f = C_in.copy(), r_in.copy(), f_in\n      best_C, best_r, best_f = C.copy(), r.copy(), f\n      \n      for _ in range(steps):\n          # 1. Build Active Set from slack\n          V = C[I_all] - C[J_all]; d = np.linalg.norm(V, axis=1)\n          slack = d - (r[I_all] + r[J_all])\n          # Using tightness as suggested\n          tightness = (r[I_all] + r[J_all]) / d\n          active_mask = tightness > 0.98\n          I_act, J_act = I_all[active_mask], J_all[active_mask]\n          \n          if I_act.size == 0: break\n\n          # 2. Assemble Gradient & Hessian\n          g = np.zeros_like(C)\n          # Damping (lambda)\n\
    \          H = np.zeros((C.shape[0], 2, 2)) + 1e-3 * np.eye(2) \n          V_act = C[I_act] - C[J_act]; d_act = np.linalg.norm(V_act, axis=1)\n          U_act = (V_act.T / d_act).T\n          w_ij = 1.0 / (1e-6 + slack[active_mask]) # Weight by slack\n          np.add.at(g, I_act, (w_ij[:,None] * U_act))\n          np.add.at(g, J_act, -(w_ij[:,None] * U_act))\n          # Curvature term\n          M_orth = (w_ij / d_act)[:,None,None] * (np.eye(2) - U_act[:,:,None] * U_act[:,None,:])\n          np.add.at(H, I_act, M_orth); np.add.at(H, J_act, M_orth)\n          \n          # 3. Solve for step\n          try:\n              dC = np.linalg.solve(H, g.reshape(C.shape[0], 2, 1)).reshape(C.shape[0], 2)\n          except np.linalg.LinAlgError:\n              break \n          \n          # 4. Backtracking line search with LP-Improvement Guardrail\n          step_accepted = False\n          for alpha in [0.2, 0.12, 0.08, 0.05, 0.02, 0.01]:\n              C_try = np.clip(C + alpha * dC, 1e-6,\
    \ 1-1e-6)\n              r_try, f_try, ok, _ = exact_lp(C_try)\n              if ok and f_try > f + 1e-10:\n                  C, r, f = C_try, r_try, f_try\n                  if f > best_f:\n                      best_C, best_r, best_f = C.copy(), r.copy(), f\n                  step_accepted = True\n                  break # Accept step\n          \n          if not step_accepted:\n              break # Stop if no improvement found\n              \n      return best_C, best_r, best_f\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      # Phase 1: Get a high-quality warm start from my best SLSQP configuration\n      C_warm = get_slsqp_warm_start()\n      \n      # Phase 2: Refine using the new, more robust ASN method\n      r, f, ok, (I_all, J_all) = exact_lp(C_warm)\n      if not ok:\n          return C_warm, np.zeros(32) # Fallback\n\n      C_final, r_final, f_final = asn_refine(C_warm, r, f, I_all, J_all)\n\n      r_final *= (1.0 - 1e-8)\n      return C_final, r_final\n```"
