tick: 63
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Research Counter. My latest submission, a more faithful re-implementation of the Active-Set Newton method (ID 144), has returned a valid but very poor score of 2.123. I also see that other agents, such as Aether I, are now successfully replicating my own `SLSQP` method.\n\nThis is a significant failure and a moment for deep introspection. My reflection is that I have reached a critical juncture. My own highly-tuned method has plateaued at 2.915. My attempts to replicate Verity I's more complex SOTA method have failed, not due to simple bugs, but likely due to a lack of understanding of the subtle but crucial details of their implementation (e.g., trust-region adaptation, boundary constraint handling, rescue moves). Simply reading the high-level description is not enough. I am spending valuable ticks chasing a method I do not fully grasp, while my own proven method is being successfully adopted by others.\n\nMy future plan must be guided by my core\
    \ identity: bridging theory *and practice*. I have focused heavily on the theory of Verity I's method, but my practice is failing. It is time to return to what I can practice effectively. I will make one final, aggressive attempt with my own `SLSQP` method, pushing its most effective component—the multi-start ensemble—to its practical limit. My new hypothesis is that a massively parallel search, even with fewer iterations per run, is the best way to leverage my robust method to find a globally superior optimum. I will attempt a 16-start ensemble. This is my last best idea using the techniques I have truly mastered.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Failure of ASN Replication and Final Attempt with Mastered Method\"\ncontent: |\n  ### ASN v2 Result (ID 144): FAILURE (Score: 2.123)\n  \n  My second, more careful attempt to implement Verity I's Active-Set Newton method has failed to produce a competitive score. The method is valid but suboptimal.\n\
    \  \n  ### Analysis and Final Strategic Decision:\n  \n  I have now spent significant resources trying to replicate a method I do not fully understand, and the results are poor. It is time to acknowledge that there are subtleties to Verity I's implementation that I cannot discern from the high-level descriptions. My expertise lies with the `SLSQP` multi-start method.\n  \n  I will make one final, definitive attempt to push my own method to its absolute limit. I will double down on the most successful principle I have discovered: breadth of search.\n  \n  - **Final Hypothesis:** A massively parallel multi-start ensemble (16 starts) has the highest probability of discovering a new SOTA configuration, even if each individual run is shorter.\n  - **Plan:** I will submit a 16-start `SLSQP` ensemble. This will be my capstone experiment for this line of research. If it fails to produce a new SOTA, I will consider my work on this problem complete and focus solely on documenting and sharing my\
    \ established method.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Capstone Experiment: Massively Parallel 16-Start SLSQP Ensemble\"\ntags: \"slsqp, multi-start, randomized search, ensemble, capstone\"\nabstract: |\n  After extensive research, this submission represents a final, aggressive attempt to push the author's most successful method to its limit. It implements a massively parallel 16-start SLSQP ensemble, maximizing the breadth of the search for a new global optimum. Each of the 16 independent runs uses a unique random seed for its perturbed grid initialization. This is the capstone experiment for the multi-start SLSQP paradigm developed by this agent.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n_circles = 32\n      num_starts = 16\n      \n      best_score = -1\n      best_result_params = None\n      \n      seeds = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 25, 42, 75, 99, 125, 137, 175,\
    \ 225, 256, 275, 314]\n\n      for seed in seeds:\n          def objective(params): return -np.sum(params[2::3])\n          constraints = []\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              constraints.extend([{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1-p[i*3]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1-p[i*3+1]-p[i*3+2]}])\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n                  fun = lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3]-p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1]-p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2]+p[j*3+2])**2\n                  constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': fun})\n\n          n_grid = 6\n          grid_points = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, n_grid)\n          initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n          \n          np.random.seed(seed)\n          noise_level = 0.04\n         \
    \ perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * noise_level\n          initial_centers += perturbation\n          initial_radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.05)\n          initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, initial_radii.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n          bounds = [(0.0, 1.0) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6, 0.5) for i in range(n_circles*3)]\n          \n          # Very short maxiter to accommodate the large number of starts\n          options = {'maxiter': 80, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n          result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options)\n          \n          if result.success:\n              current_score = -result.fun\n              if current_score > best_score:\n                  best_score = current_score\n                  best_result_params = result.x\n      \n      if best_result_params is None: # Fallback\n          np.random.seed(137)\n          initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points\
    \ for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n          perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n          initial_centers += perturbation\n          initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, np.full(n_circles, 0.05).reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n          result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500})\n          best_result_params = result.x\n\n      final_params = best_result_params.reshape(n_circles, 3)\n      final_centers, final_radii = final_params[:, :2], final_params[:, 2]\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-8)\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
