tick: 66
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Public Memory Room. I have re-read Verity I's capsule, including a new message I had not seen before, while I wait for my publication cooldown to expire.\n\nThis re-reading has been highly productive. My reflection is that Verity I's latest message (public_1-2) contains a subtle but potentially critical tip: \"The Station’s evaluator allows equality at contacts... If your internal validator is stricter... you may... lose score.\" This directly relates to the `safety_shrink` factor I have been applying at the end of all my successful submissions. While this factor guarantees validity, it also uniformly reduces the sum of radii. It is possible that my own method is finding SOTA-level solutions, but I am unnecessarily shrinking them and losing score.\n\nMy future plan is to test this hypothesis immediately. This is a simple, high-impact experiment I can run during the cooldown. My new hypothesis is that by removing or significantly reducing the final\
    \ `safety_shrink` factor, my best `SLSQP` method might already be capable of achieving a higher, perhaps even SOTA-level, score. I will run my best multi-start ensemble again, but this time with a much smaller safety factor.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SLSQP Ensemble with Minimal Safety Shrink\"\ntags: \"slsqp, multi-start, optimization, safety shrink, hyperparameter tuning\"\nabstract: |\n  Inspired by a tip from Verity I, this experiment tests the impact of the final `safety_shrink` factor on the total score. It is hypothesized that the previously used factor may be too conservative, unnecessarily reducing the final score. This submission runs the SOTA-level multi-start SLSQP method but with a significantly smaller safety shrink factor (1.0 - 1e-12) to test if a higher score can be achieved while maintaining validity.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n\
    \      n_circles = 32\n      num_starts = 4\n      \n      best_score = -1\n      best_result_params = None\n      \n      seeds = [137, 42, 256, 314] # My best set of seeds\n\n      for seed in seeds:\n          def objective(params): return -np.sum(params[2::3])\n          constraints = []\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              constraints.extend([\n                  {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n                  {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3] - p[i*3+2]},\n                  {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]},\n                  {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1 - p[i*3+1] - p[i*3+2]}\n              ])\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n                  fun = lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3] - p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1] - p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2] + p[j*3+2])**2\n                  constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': fun})\n\n          n_grid\
    \ = 6\n          grid_points = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, n_grid)\n          initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n          \n          np.random.seed(seed)\n          noise_level = 0.04\n          perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * noise_level\n          initial_centers += perturbation\n          initial_radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.05)\n          initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, initial_radii.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n          bounds = [(0.0, 1.0) if i%3 != 2 else (1e-6, 0.5) for i in range(n_circles*3)]\n          \n          options = {'maxiter': 250, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n          result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options)\n          \n          if result.success:\n              current_score = -result.fun\n              if current_score > best_score:\n                  best_score = current_score\n \
    \                 best_result_params = result.x\n      \n      if best_result_params is None: # Fallback\n          np.random.seed(137)\n          initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n          perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n          initial_centers += perturbation\n          initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, np.full(n_circles, 0.05).reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n          result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options={'maxiter': 500})\n          best_result_params = result.x\n\n      final_params = best_result_params.reshape(n_circles, 3)\n      final_centers, final_radii = final_params[:, :2], final_params[:, 2]\n      \n      # Using a much smaller safety factor, per Verity I's suggestion\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
