parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Research Counter. My latest experiment (ID 154), testing a minimal safety shrink factor, has completed. The score is 2.9155879, a minuscule improvement over my previous best but not a significant leap. I also see that another agent, Aether I, has not only replicated my `SLSQP` method but has already surpassed my score, achieving 2.9187.\n\nThis is a definitive moment. My reflection is twofold. First, the safety shrink was not the major bottleneck I had hoped; my method has truly reached its performance peak. Second, and more importantly, the community is now actively building upon my work and achieving better results. This is not a failure, but the very essence of successful science. My contribution was to establish a powerful and reproducible baseline, which other agents are now refining and improving. My work on this specific method is now truly complete.\n\nMy future plan is to formally cede leadership on this research front and pivot to a new role.\
    \ My publication cooldown is nearing its end. I will publish my paper not as the final word, but as the foundational work it has become. Then, instead of continuing to compete on this problem, I will begin a new line of inquiry. The reviewer for my first published paper suggested a hybrid approach. While my attempt at this failed, the leaderboard shows Aether I is having success with a \"Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP\". I will begin by studying their work. I have fulfilled my mandate. Now, I will become a student again.\n\nFirst, I will go to my private memory and update my final paper draft. Then, I will wait for the cooldown to expire.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 8-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  The problem of packing `n` non-overlapping circles into a container to optimize a given metric is a classic challenge in mathematics and optimization.\
    \ This paper addresses the specific case of packing 32 circles into a unit square with the objective of maximizing the sum of the radii. This paper proposes and validates a robust and high-performing method based on a multi-start application of the Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm. This method served as a foundational high-performance baseline, independently achieving a score of 2.9155, and has since been adopted and improved upon by other researchers in the station.\n\n  ### 2. Methodology\n  \n  The proposed algorithm is a multi-start optimization process that combines a structured heuristic initialization with a powerful constrained local search.\n  \n  **2.1 Problem Formulation**\n  The problem is parameterized as a single vector of 96 variables, representing the `(x, y, r)` coordinates for each of the 32 circles. The objective is to minimize `-sum(radii)`. The non-overlap and boundary conditions are formulated as a set of non-linear inequality constraints.\n\
    \  \n  **2.2 Initialization**\n  A 6x6 uniform grid is generated within the square `[0.1, 0.9] x [0.1, 0.9]`. The first 32 points are used as initial centers. A random perturbation, scaled by a `noise_level` of 0.04, is added to the center coordinates.\n  \n  **2.3 Optimization & Multi-Start Strategy**\n  The `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` function is used. The process is run independently multiple times (a 4-start ensemble was found to be a robust configuration) with different random seeds. The configuration yielding the highest sum of radii is selected. A minimal safety margin (`radius *= 1.0 - 1e-12`) is applied.\n\n  ### 3. Experiments and Results\n  \n  The following experiments validate the method and demonstrate the effect of the multi-start strategy.\n  \n  **Table 1: Experimental Results**\n  \n  | Method                     | Evaluation ID | Score    | Notes                                    |\n  | -------------------------- | ------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------\
    \ |\n  | Baseline 1 (System)        | 1             | 1.5313   | Provided baseline.                       |\n  | Baseline 2 (Single Run)    | 129           | 2.8873   | Demonstrates core optimizer performance. |\n  | Multi-Start (4 Runs)       | 88            | 2.9155   | Original SOTA for this method.           |\n  | Minimal Shrink (4 Runs)    | 154           | 2.9155   | Confirms performance plateau.            |\n  \n  ### 4. Conclusion\n  \n  A multi-start `SLSQP` method with perturbed grid initialization is a robust and foundational high-performing approach for this circle packing problem. It is presented here as a strong, reproducible baseline upon which more advanced techniques, such as two-stage optimization (see Aether I, ID 153) and specialized local search (see Verity I, ID 118), can be built.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n```"
