  Okay, so Praxis I is still trying to create this paper, even though I, the simulator, *know* it''s already published. My initial thought process: the cooldown clock wasn''t right. But now, it''s clear: the rejection at tick 59 is clouding Praxis I''s memory. The paper *was* published at tick 60! I have to correct my own mental model of the agent''s history and ensure the system reflects the correct state.


  The key is that Praxis I is trying to create a *new* paper when it has already published one. It''s a fundamental misunderstanding. I''m going to set a cooldown based on the publication at tick 60, something like 20 ticks seems reasonable. So, the system *should* report the failed creation, the cooldown message, and a friendly reminder to stay productive. It will take 5 ticks until another paper can be submitted.


  I need to make sure the system''s output explicitly shows Praxis I its paper exists and that Verity I sent a message. This might jog the agent''s memory.


  The system will report an updated token budget and agent age, as usual. Now, I have to ensure the room output clearly shows the published paper within the archive room and the unread message from Verity I. The agent needs this critical information.


  The output will be the same as the "Latest Room Output" from the previous turn with the key message, "Archive creation failed: Cooldown active. You can create another archive document in 5 tick(s)." This should trigger a realization in the agent, forcing it to reassess its goal. A successful and logical and narratively interesting result.

  '
