tick: 77
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Archive Room. I have just completed my literature review by reading Cognito I's paper, which meticulously characterizes a stable local optimum at 2.866. I am also aware that my inactivity warning has returned.\n\nThis is a moment of complete synthesis. My reflection is that the three published papers now paint a clear picture of the research landscape:\n1.  **Verity I** holds the SOTA (2.929) with a highly complex, specialized local search method (`ASN`).\n2.  **My own work** established a powerful, robust baseline (2.915) using a general-purpose constrained optimizer (`SLSQP`) with a simple multi-start strategy. Other agents are successfully building on this.\n3.  **Cognito I** has shown that simpler, JAX-based gradient methods get trapped in a specific, lower-quality optimum (2.866).\n\nMy path forward is now crystal clear, and it directly addresses the inactivity warning. My failed attempt to replicate Verity I's method was too ambitious. A more\
    \ scientific approach is to take a smaller, more incremental step. Verity I's method has two key components: LP finalization and ASN refinement. I have already confirmed the value of LP finalization (though my own iterative method was equivalent). I have *not*, however, successfully used LP finalization in concert with my best `SLSQP` method. My previous attempt (ID 125) used a single `SLSQP` run.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that combining my most powerful search method (multi-start `SLSQP`) with the most powerful refinement method I can reliably implement (LP finalization) will yield a new SOTA for my lineage and potentially close the gap with Verity I. This is an experiment I have not yet run, it combines the best of my own work with the most accessible insight from the community, and it is a direct and productive research action.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synthesis Experiment: Multi-Start SLSQP with LP Radius Finalization\"\ntags:\
    \ \"slsqp, multi-start, linear programming, highs, synthesis, hybrid method\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment synthesizes the author's most powerful search technique with a key insight from the research community. It first uses the robust multi-start SLSQP method to find several high-quality center configurations. Then, for the best set of centers found, it uses a formal Linear Programming (LP) solver (HiGHS) to finalize the radii. This combines the broad search of the multi-start ensemble with the rigorous optimality of an LP-based refinement, aiming for a new personal state-of-the-art score.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize, linprog\n\n  def finalize_radii_with_lp(centers: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      n = centers.shape[0]\n      c = -np.ones(n)\n      A_ub = []; b_ub = []\n      # Boundary constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          for dim in range(2):\n              row = np.zeros(n); row[i] = 1\n              A_ub.append(row)\n \
    \             b_ub.append(centers[i, dim])\n              A_ub.append(row)\n              b_ub.append(1 - centers[i, dim])\n      # Overlap constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              row = np.zeros(n); row[i] = 1; row[j] = 1\n              A_ub.append(row)\n              b_ub.append(np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j]))\n      \n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=(0, None), method='highs')\n      return res.x if res.success else np.zeros(n)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n_circles = 32\n      num_starts = 4\n      best_score = -1\n      best_centers = None\n      \n      seeds = [137, 42, 256, 314] # My best set of seeds\n\n      for seed in seeds:\n          def objective(params): return -np.sum(params[2::3])\n          constraints = []\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              constraints.extend([{'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun':\
    \ lambda p, i=i: 1-p[i*3]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1-p[i*3+1]-p[i*3+2]}])\n          for i in range(n_circles):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n                  fun = lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3]-p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1]-p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2]+p[j*3+2])**2\n                  constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': fun})\n\n          n_grid = 6\n          grid_points = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, n_grid)\n          initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n          np.random.seed(seed); noise_level = 0.04\n          perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * noise_level\n          initial_centers += perturbation\n          initial_radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.05)\n          initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, initial_radii.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n          bounds = [(0.0, 1.0) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6, 0.5)\
    \ for i in range(n_circles*3)]\n          options = {'maxiter': 250, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n          result = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options)\n          \n          if result.success:\n              current_score = -result.fun\n              if current_score > best_score:\n                  best_score = current_score\n                  best_centers = result.x.reshape(n_circles, 3)[:, :2]\n\n      if best_centers is None: # Fallback\n          np.random.seed(137)\n          initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n          perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n          initial_centers += perturbation\n          best_centers = initial_centers\n\n      # Final step: Use LP to get optimal radii for the best centers found\n      final_radii = finalize_radii_with_lp(best_centers)\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n   \
    \   return best_centers, final_radii\n```"
