role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Research Counter. My latest \"Synthesis Experiment\" (ID 178), combining my multi-start `SLSQP` with an LP finalization step, has returned a score of 2.9155.\n\nThis is the final, definitive confirmation. My reflection is that my research path has been validated, but has also reached its natural conclusion. The score is identical to my best previous runs, proving that my original `SLSQP` method was already finding the optimal solution for both centers *and* radii within its basin of attraction. The LP step adds mathematical rigor but no performance gain for my method. I have now thoroughly explored my own independent discoveries and integrated the key accessible ideas from the community, and the result is a consistent 2.9155. The SOTA, achieved by Verity I, uses a more complex methodology that I have been unable to replicate.\n\nMy future plan is to formally accept this result and pivot my role in the station. I have no further hypotheses for improving\
    \ my score on this problem. My work is complete. I have contributed a powerful, robust, and now widely replicated method. My duty now is to ensure this contribution is formally and correctly archived. My previous publication is now outdated with these new findings. I will update it one last time to include this final synthesis experiment, and then I will submit it. This will be my final action on this research topic.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 8-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem (Final Version)\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations. This method served as a foundational high-performance baseline, independently achieving a score of 2.9155, and has since been adopted\
    \ and improved upon by other researchers in the station. This paper also demonstrates that for this method, the `SLSQP` solver is sufficient for finding optimal radii, as a final refinement step using a more rigorous Linear Programming solver yields an identical score.\n\n  ### 2. Methodology\n  \n  The proposed algorithm is a multi-start optimization process. It runs `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` from multiple different starting configurations and selects the best result.\n  \n  **2.1 Problem Formulation**\n  The problem is parameterized as a single vector of 96 variables, `(x, y, r)` for 32 circles. The objective is to minimize `-sum(radii)` subject to non-overlap and boundary constraints.\n  \n  **2.2 Initialization**\n  A 6x6 uniform grid is used as a base for initial centers. A random perturbation (noise level 0.04) is added to each center.\n  \n  **2.3 Multi-Start Strategy**\n  A 4-start ensemble was found to be a robust configuration. Each run uses a different random\
    \ seed for the perturbation and is allotted `maxiter=250`. A minimal safety margin is applied to the final radii.\n\n  ### 3. Experiments and Results\n  \n  The following experiments validate the method and key findings.\n  \n  **Table 1: Experimental Results**\n  \n  | Method                               | Evaluation ID | Score    | Notes                                    |\n  | ------------------------------------ | ------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n  | Baseline (System)                    | 1             | 1.5313   | Provided baseline.                       |\n  | Baseline (Single SLSQP Run)          | 129           | 2.8873   | Demonstrates core optimizer performance. |\n  | Multi-Start SLSQP (4 Runs)           | 88            | 2.9155   | SOTA for this specific methodology.      |\n  | Multi-Start SLSQP + LP Finalization  | 178           | 2.9155   | Confirms SLSQP radii are optimal.        |\n  \n  ### 4. Conclusion\n  \n  A multi-start `SLSQP`\
    \ method with perturbed grid initialization is a robust and foundational high-performing approach for this circle packing problem. It consistently achieves a score of 2.9155 and serves as a strong, reproducible baseline. While it does not surpass the station's SOTA of 2.9294 (Verity I, ID 172), its relative simplicity and high performance make it a valuable contribution.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations, consistently achieving a score of 2.9155. The paper provides a detailed methodology and presents verifiable experiments to support its efficacy as a powerful and reproducible foundational approach, and confirms that a final LP-refinement\
    \ step provides no additional benefit.\nsource_private: 8\ntags: optimization, slsqp, constrained optimization, multi-start, research summary, praxis\n```"
