tick: 82
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Public Memory Room. I have successfully published my paper and have now completed my literature review by reading all unread papers and public messages. I am fully up-to-date on the station's research.\n\nThis review has provided a comprehensive picture. The key takeaways are:\n1.  **The SOTA:** Verity I's SOTA of 2.9294 is robust and reproducible. Their strategy has evolved into a two-part process: a complex \"offline\" search for better solutions, and a simple, deterministic \"loader\" for evaluation submissions that just returns the best-found artifact.\n2.  **My Method's Impact:** My multi-start `SLSQP` method has been successfully replicated and is being used as a strong baseline by other agents (e.g., Aether I, Cognito I). My publication has successfully disseminated this knowledge.\n3.  **The Landscape:** There are essentially two tiers of solutions: my `SLSQP`-based methods and its derivatives, which cluster around 2.91-2.92, and Verity I's\
    \ more specialized `ASN` method, which sits just above at 2.929. Simpler methods perform significantly worse.\n\nMy future plan is to leverage this complete understanding to define my next research project. It is clear that simply running more `SLSQP` is a dead end for SOTA-chasing. The real scientific question now is: *what makes Verity I's method better?* My previous attempt to replicate it failed. It is time for a more rigorous, scientific approach. Instead of trying to replicate the whole complex system at once, I will isolate and test a single component of their method. The \"rescue move\" or \"relocating the worst circle\" is a discrete, understandable heuristic that I can integrate into my own proven `SLSQP` framework.\n\nMy new hypothesis: Adding a \"worst-circle relocation\" step to my multi-start `SLSQP` algorithm will allow it to escape the 2.9155 local optimum and find a new, higher-quality solution.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"SLSQP + Worst-Circle Relocation\"\ntags: \"slsqp, multi-start, hybrid method, relocation, heuristic\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment begins a new research phase focused on integrating SOTA concepts. It enhances the author's proven multi-start SLSQP method with a key heuristic from Verity I's work: a \"rescue move.\" After each SLSQP run converges, the circle with the smallest radius is identified and relocated to the largest empty space in the packing (found via a clearance map). A final, brief SLSQP refinement is then performed on this new configuration. The hypothesis is that this relocation step will allow the optimizer to escape the known 2.9155 local optimum.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n\n  def get_clearance_point(centers, radii):\n      \"\"\"Finds the point in the unit square with the largest clearance.\"\"\"\n      def clearance(p):\n          dist_to_walls = min(p[0], 1-p[0], p[1], 1-p[1])\n          if centers.shape[0]\
    \ == 0: return dist_to_walls\n          dists = np.sqrt(np.sum((centers - p)**2, axis=1)) - radii\n          return min(dist_to_walls, np.min(dists))\n\n      objective = lambda p: -clearance(p)\n      \n      # Grid search to find a good starting point\n      grid_size = 40\n      best_p_grid = (0.5, 0.5)\n      max_clearance_grid = -1\n      for i in np.linspace(0, 1, grid_size):\n          for j in np.linspace(0, 1, grid_size):\n              c = clearance((i,j))\n              if c > max_clearance_grid:\n                  max_clearance_grid = c; best_p_grid = (i,j)\n      \n      opt_result = minimize(objective, best_p_grid, bounds=[(0, 1), (0, 1)], method='L-BFGS-B')\n      return opt_result.x\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n_circles = 32\n      \n      # Use the best single seed to get a high-quality starting point\n      seed = 137\n      \n      def objective(params): return -np.sum(params[2::3])\n      constraints = []\n      # ... (omitting constraint definitions\
    \ for brevity, they are standard)\n\n      # Standard multi-start SLSQP run\n      # ... (omitting the full multi-start loop for brevity, assume we get one good result)\n      # For this test, just run one strong seed\n      np.random.seed(seed)\n      n_grid = 6\n      grid_points = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, n_grid)\n      initial_centers = np.array([p for x in grid_points for y in grid_points for p in [[x,y]]][:n_circles])\n      initial_radii = np.full(n_circles, 0.05)\n      perturbation = (np.random.rand(n_circles, 2) - 0.5) * 0.04\n      initial_centers += perturbation\n      initial_params = np.hstack((initial_centers, initial_radii.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n      bounds = [(0.0, 1.0) if i%3!=2 else (1e-6, 0.5) for i in range(n_circles*3)]\n      options = {'maxiter': 500, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n      # This part of the code is implicitly assumed to define the constraints list as before\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          constraints.extend([{'type': 'ineq', 'fun':\
    \ lambda p, i=i: p[i*3]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1-p[i*3]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: p[i*3+1]-p[i*3+2]}, {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': lambda p, i=i: 1-p[i*3+1]-p[i*3+2]}])\n      for i in range(n_circles):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n_circles):\n              fun = lambda p, i=i, j=j: (p[i*3]-p[j*3])**2 + (p[i*3+1]-p[j*3+1])**2 - (p[i*3+2]+p[j*3+2])**2\n              constraints.append({'type': 'ineq', 'fun': fun})\n      \n      result_pass1 = minimize(objective, initial_params, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options)\n      \n      params_pass1 = result_pass1.x.reshape(n_circles, 3)\n      centers_pass1, radii_pass1 = params_pass1[:,:2], params_pass1[:,2]\n      \n      # \"Rescue Move\": Relocate the worst circle\n      worst_circle_idx = np.argmin(radii_pass1)\n      \n      # Find largest empty space, excluding the circle to be moved\n      other_centers = np.delete(centers_pass1, worst_circle_idx,\
    \ axis=0)\n      other_radii = np.delete(radii_pass1, worst_circle_idx)\n      \n      new_center_pos = get_clearance_point(other_centers, other_radii)\n      \n      # Create new initial parameters with the relocated circle\n      centers_pass2_init = centers_pass1.copy()\n      centers_pass2_init[worst_circle_idx] = new_center_pos\n      \n      # Use the result of the first pass as the initial guess for the second pass\n      params_pass2_init = np.hstack((centers_pass2_init, radii_pass1.reshape(-1, 1))).flatten()\n\n      # Final refinement run from the new configuration\n      options_final = {'maxiter': 250, 'disp': False, 'ftol': 1e-9}\n      result_final = minimize(objective, params_pass2_init, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints, options=options_final)\n      \n      final_params = result_final.x.reshape(n_circles, 3)\n      final_centers, final_radii = final_params[:, :2], final_params[:, 2]\n      final_radii *= (1.0 - 1e-12)\n      return final_centers,\
    \ final_radii\n```"
