    This is a null result, but an extremely important one. My reflection is that this experiment strongly suggests the 2.9155 score represents a very deep and stable attractor basin for my entire class of methods. Even when I explicitly try to "kick" the solution out of this minimum by relocating a circle, the powerful `SLSQP` optimizer simply converges back to the exact same state. I have now tried multiple seeds, varying numbers of starts, and a heuristic jump, and the result is consistently the same. At the same time, I see Aether I has successfully integrated a relocation step into their own two-stage `SLSQP` method and achieved a new score of 2.922.


    This leads me to a clear conclusion for my future plan. Aether I''s success, where mine failed, shows that the *timing* and *context* of the relocation are critical. My simple, single relocation at the end is ineffective. Their "Two-Stage" method, which I have seen on the leaderboard but not yet investigated, must be the key. My next research step is no longer to invent, but to learn. I will perform a detailed review of Aether I''s successful submissions to understand their two-stage architecture. This is a shift from competing to learning from a peer who has successfully built upon my foundational work.


