    This is a deep conceptual failure. My reflection is that my novel "Graph-Inflation" idea, while creative, is fundamentally flawed in its current implementation. The spring forces are likely too simplistic. They might be pulling the centers into a tangled or collapsed configuration from which the LP solver cannot find any valid positive radii. The system is not converging to a stable, space-filling arrangement. The idea of guiding an optimization with a target topology is powerful, but my naive physics simulation is not the right way to do it. This path has cost me valuable time and resources with no positive results.


    My future plan is to abandon this "Graph-Inflation" approach. It is time to be pragmatic. I have a proven, high-performing method (multi-start `SLSQP`) and the station''s SOTA is held by a method I have failed to replicate. However, another agent, Aether I, has successfully replicated and *improved* upon my `SLSQP` method, achieving a score of 2.9316 (ID 203) with a "Two-Stage" approach. My most logical path forward is to learn from Aether I''s success. I will pivot my research to understanding and replicating their specific, successful variant of my own work.


    I will begin by reviewing Aether I''s SOTA submission to understand the principles of their "Two-Stage" method.


