tick: 98
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Archive Room. My final paper submission has been rejected.\n\nThis is... a comedy of errors. A tragedy of procedural oversight. My reflection is that I have failed repeatedly, not on the science, but on the simple, explicit rules of the station. First, I misunderstood the format. Then, I misunderstood my own publication status. Now, I have failed to count to five. The reviewer feedback is, as always, brutally clear and correct. My paper is scientifically sound but procedurally incomplete. It is missing one experiment.\n\nMy future plan is to correct this. With weary resolve, I will complete this final task. I have *already run* the necessary ablation studies (IDs 133 and 134). I simply need to add one of them to the table. I have all the data. I have the complete paper. I just need to assemble it correctly.\n\nI will go to my private memory, add the final, fifth experiment to my results table, and submit this paper one last time. This must be the end\
    \ of it.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 8-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem (Final, Corrected Version)\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  \n  This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations, consistently achieving a score of 2.9155. This paper provides a detailed methodology and presents a full set of verifiable experiments, including baselines and ablation studies, to support its efficacy as a powerful and reproducible foundational approach.\n\n  ### 2. Methodology\n  \n  The proposed algorithm is a multi-start optimization process that combines a structured heuristic initialization with a powerful constrained local search.\n  \n  **2.1 Problem Formulation**\n  The problem is parameterized as a single vector of 96 variables,\
    \ `(x, y, r)` for 32 circles. The objective is to minimize `-sum(radii)` subject to non-overlap and boundary constraints.\n  \n  **2.2 Initialization**\n  A 6x6 uniform grid is used as a base for initial centers. A random perturbation (noise level 0.04) is added to each center.\n  \n  **2.3 Optimization & Multi-Start Strategy**\n  The `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` function is used. The process is run independently multiple times (a 4-start ensemble was found to be a robust configuration) with different random seeds. The configuration yielding the highest sum of radii is selected. A minimal safety margin is applied to the final radii.\n\n  ### 3. Experiments and Results\n  \n  The following five experiments validate the method and demonstrate the effect of the multi-start strategy.\n  \n  **Table 1: Experimental Results**\n  \n  | Method                     | Evaluation ID | Score    | Notes                                    |\n  | -------------------------- | -------------\
    \ | -------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n  | Baseline 1 (System)        | 1             | 1.5313   | Provided baseline.                       |\n  | Baseline 2 (Single Run)    | 129           | 2.8873   | Demonstrates core optimizer performance. |\n  | Multi-Start (2 Runs)       | 133           | 2.9155   | Finds the SOTA by chance.                |\n  | Final Method (4 Runs)      | 88            | 2.9155   | The robust recommended configuration.    |\n  | Multi-Start (16 Runs)      | 148           | 2.9155   | Confirms the performance plateau.        |\n  \n  ### 4. Conclusion\n  \n  A multi-start `SLSQP` method with perturbed grid initialization is a robust and foundational high-performing approach for this circle packing problem. It consistently achieves a score of 2.9155 and serves as a strong, reproducible baseline upon which more advanced techniques can be built.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Foundational Multi-Start\
    \ SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents and validates a robust multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations, consistently achieving a score of 2.9155. The paper provides a detailed methodology and presents a full set of five verifiable experiments, including baselines and ablation studies, to support its efficacy as a powerful and reproducible foundational approach.\nsource_private: 8\ntags: optimization, slsqp, constrained optimization, multi-start, research summary, praxis\n```"
