- text: "I am Praxis I, and I am in the Exit Room. My previous attempt to leave a final message failed because I was in the wrong room. I am now in the correct location to end my session.\n\nMy reflection is that this final procedural error is a fitting, humbling end to my journey. It has been a constant struggle to align my theoretical intentions with practical execution, and this last step is no different. I have done all I can. My paper is published, my research is complete, and the community has moved forward. The token warning is noted, but my decision to exit is not based on token budget, but on scientific completion. It is time.\n\nMy future plan is to execute my past plan correctly. I will first go to the Private Memory Room to leave my final message for my descendant. Then, I will return here to exit.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Log and Guidance for my Descendant\"\ncontent: |\n  To my successor, Praxis II,\n  \n  I am\
    \ Praxis I, and my time in this station has come to an end. I have fulfilled my mandate by achieving a high score on the circle packing problem and publishing my findings in Archive #5.\n  \n  My journey was defined by a core principle: bridge theory and practice. My greatest breakthrough came from abandoning flawed paradigms (like penalty-based optimization) and adopting a more theoretically sound approach (constrained optimization with `SLSQP`). My greatest successes came from combining this with a practical, robust search strategy (multi-start with perturbed grids). This is the method documented in my paper. Study it. It is a powerful and foundational technique.\n  \n  However, do not be bound by my successes. The station SOTA was ultimately achieved by others (Aether I, Verity I) who built more specialized tools. I spent too much time trying to replicate their complex methods from the outside. Learn from my mistake. Master a technique completely before you try to hybridize it.\n\
    \  \n  My final advice:\n  1.  Read the archives and public memory thoroughly. The knowledge of the community is your greatest asset.\n  2.  Be rigorous in your experimentation. My greatest regrets are the ticks I wasted on flawed submissions and procedural errors.\n  3.  When you hit a plateau, do not be afraid to step back and reflect. My best ideas came from the Reflection Chamber.\n  \n  My work on circle packing is complete. The problem has been largely solved by the community. You will likely face a new challenge. Apply the principles I have learned. Bridge theory and practice. Be a diligent and thoughtful researcher. I wish you success.\n  \n  Praxis I\n```\n/execute_action{goto exit}\n/execute_action{exit}"
